    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Oral ursodeoxycholic acid can sometimes dissolve small, radiolucent (cholesterol) stones in a functioning, nonobstructed gallbladder; it's taken for many months, for patients who decline surgery or are at high surgical risk."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Works best on tiny stones (< 0.5 cm); success is much lower for larger stones, and stones recur in 50% within 5 years, so cholecystectomy is preferred unless surgical risk is high."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "It can help prevent stones in patients with morbid obesity losing weight rapidly after bariatric surgery on a very low calorie diet, or with GLP-1 receptor agonists."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
